    "q-154000": {
      "dom_id": "step-q154000",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Solve the inequality $-5x<10.$",
      "worked_solution": "The variable $x$ is being *multiplied* by $-5.$ To isolate $x,$ we perform the *opposite* operation, which is *dividing* by $-5.$ Remember to perform the same operation on both sides.\n\nAlso, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5x\\,\\, & <\\,\\,10 \\\\ \\frac{-5x}{-5}\\,\\, & >\\,\\,\\frac{10}{-5} \\\\ \\frac{-5x}{-5}\\,\\, & >\\,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $-5$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-5x}{-5} & >-2 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-5}x}{\\cancel{-5}} & >-2 \\\\ x & >-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x>-2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
