    "q-181189": {
      "dom_id": "step-q181189",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "The solution to the inequality $-\\frac{y}{3}\\le 4$ is $y${{field-1}}{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The variable $y$ is being *divided* by $3.$ To isolate $y$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-3.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{3}\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,4 \\\\ (-3)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{3})\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,(-3)\\cdot 4 \\\\ (-3)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{3})\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{3})\\,\\, & \\ge -12 \\\\ (\\cancel{-}3)\\cdot (\\cancel{-}\\frac{y}{3}) & \\ge -12 \\\\ 3\\cdot \\frac{y}{3} & \\ge -12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $3$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot \\frac{y}{3} & \\ge -12 \\\\ \\frac{3y}{3} & \\ge -12 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{3}y}{\\cancel{3}} & \\ge -12 \\\\ y & \\ge -12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y\\ge -12.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
