    "q-154011": {
      "dom_id": "step-q154011",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Solve the inequality $-\\frac{y}{4}\\ge -8.$",
      "worked_solution": "The variable $y$ is being *divided* by $4.$ To isolate $y$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-4.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{4}\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,-8 \\\\ (-4)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{4})\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,(-4)\\cdot (-8) \\\\ (-4)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{4})\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{4}) & \\le 32 \\\\ (\\cancel{-}4)\\cdot (\\cancel{-}\\frac{y}{4}) & \\le 32 \\\\ 4\\cdot \\frac{y}{4} & \\le 32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $4$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot \\frac{y}{4} & \\le 32 \\\\ \\frac{4y}{4} & \\le 32 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{4}y}{\\cancel{4}} & \\le 32 \\\\ y & \\le 32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y\\le 32.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
